1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Every culture on Earth agrees on one thing
about the missing.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Somebody should have left a note.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
A ship should come with a log.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
A settlement should come with a letter.

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
A man found on a beach should come with a
wallet, and ideally a name.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
But history keeps a small, stubborn
collection of cases where the paperwork

7
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:21,000
simply stops.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,000
No note.

9
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:23,000
No wallet.

10
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Just a quiet deck, an empty room, a single
word carved into a post.

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
And here is the part nobody warns about.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The truth behind these stories is usually
stranger, and quieter, than the legend.

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The famous version has ghosts and warm
cups of tea.

14
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,500
The record has a missing lifeboat, a slate
with half a day written on it, and a scrap

15
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:44,000
of paper that says the end.

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Six cases.

17
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,167
Real names, real dates, and the exact
places where the legend and the record

18
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:53,000
quietly go their separate ways.

19
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Here is the central claim.

20
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
The creepiest mysteries in history are not
the ones with monsters.

21
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
They are the ones where almost everything
is ordinary, and one piece refuses to fit.

22
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
That is unusual, because mysteries are
supposed to be loud.

23
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,000
They are supposed to come with curses and
shadows and a dramatic thunderclap.

24
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,042
Instead, these come with cargo manifests,
weather conditions, and a court in

25
00:01:16,042 --> 00:01:19,000
Gibraltar with a great deal of paperwork.

26
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Some will say this is overthinking it.

27
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Ships get into trouble.

28
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Waves hit lighthouses.

29
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,000
People die in the mountains.

30
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
All true.

31
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
And in several of these cases, a sensible
explanation exists.

32
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
But a sensible explanation and a finished
story are two different things.

33
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Keep that in mind.

34
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Because the first case involves a ship
that did nothing wrong, and nobody on it.

35
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:41,000
December 1872.

36
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The middle of the Atlantic, roughly
halfway between the Azores and Portugal.

37
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,500
The crew of a ship called the Dei Gratia
spots another vessel behaving strangely,

38
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:56,000
and spends a couple of hours trying to
hail it.

39
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Nobody answers.

40
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
A boarding party climbs aboard the Mary
Celeste.

41
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
The sails are partly set.

42
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,708
The cargo, more than seventeen hundred
barrels of industrial alcohol, is mostly

43
00:02:08,708 --> 00:02:09,000
fine.

44
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The lifeboat is gone.

45
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,125
Captain Benjamin Briggs, his wife, their
two-year-old daughter, and seven crew

46
00:02:15,125 --> 00:02:17,000
members are gone with it.

47
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
The last entry in the log is dated
November twenty-fifth.

48
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Ten days earlier.

49
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
This is where the legend takes over.

50
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
In 1884,

51
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,750
a young ship's surgeon named Arthur Conan
Doyle wrote a short story about a ghost

52
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:30,000
ship.

53
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,000
It was fiction.

54
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
He even changed the names.

55
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,750
Readers believed it anyway, and later
retellings kept improving it, until the

56
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:40,000
story had a hot breakfast on the table and
tea that was still warm.

57
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Nothing in the record supports any of
that.

58
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
The boarding party spent hours just trying
to reach the ship in the first place.

59
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Nobody's tea survives that.

60
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
What the record does say is odder in a
quieter way.

61
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,500
A court in Gibraltar spent months
suspecting the crew of the Dei Gratia of

62
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:02,000
foul play, and in the end found no
evidence of

63
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:03,000
it.

64
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The stains on the captain's sword turned
out to be rust.

65
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,250
The most likely explanation is that Briggs
believed his ship was in danger, perhaps

66
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:15,000
from the cargo or from water in the hold,
and

67
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
ordered everyone into the lifeboat.

68
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
That is a theory.

69
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,000
The lifeboat was never found.

70
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
A ship with nobody on it is unsettling.

71
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
A town with everybody in it is worse.

72
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,000
July 1518. Strasbourg.

73
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
A woman known as Frau Troffea steps into
the street and begins to dance.

74
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,000
No music.

75
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,000
No occasion.

76
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
She keeps going for days.

77
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,583
The chroniclers say others joined her, and
that within about a month, as many as four

78
00:03:41,583 --> 00:03:43,000
hundred people were dancing.

79
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,625
The city authorities considered their
options and decided the cure was more

80
00:03:47,625 --> 00:03:48,000
dancing.

81
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,000
This did not help.

82
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:53,000
Here is where the popular version and the
record part ways.

83
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The popular version has people dropping
dead by the dozen, fifteen a day.

84
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,333
But historians point out that the accounts
mentioning deaths come from later

85
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:05,000
retellings, not from the earliest sources.

86
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
What is solidly documented is the dancing.

87
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,000
A city in the middle of famine and
disease, watching its own neighbors unable

88
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,000
to stop.

89
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Ergot, a grain mold, is the usual suspect.

90
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
But it tends to restrict movement, not
power someone through days of dancing.

91
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,667
The historian John Waller argues for
something harder to hear: mass stress, in

92
00:04:25,667 --> 00:04:30,000
a place already terrified of a curse from
Saint Vitus.

93
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Which means the cause may not have been a
poison at all.

94
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It may have been fear, passed along like a
cold.

95
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
At least Strasbourg had witnesses.

96
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:42,000
The next case had a lighthouse.

97
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,667
December 1900. Eilean Mor, the largest of
the Flannan Isles, about twenty miles west

98
00:04:46,667 --> 00:04:49,000
of the Isle of Lewis in Scotland.

99
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The lighthouse has been operating for
barely more than a year.

100
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Three men are on duty.

101
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
James Ducat, Thomas Marshall, and Donald
MacArthur.

102
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,833
On the night of December fifteenth, a
passing steamer called the Archtor notices

103
00:05:00,833 --> 00:05:02,000
the light is out.

104
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
The steamer reports it when it reaches
port.

105
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
That report never reaches the people who
needed it in time.

106
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:14,458
Eleven days later, on December
twenty-sixth, the relief ship Hesperus

107
00:05:14,458 --> 00:05:15,000
arrives.

108
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
There is no flag.

109
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,000
There is no answer to the horn.

110
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Relief keeper Joseph Moore goes ashore and
finds the lighthouse empty.

111
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
The record is dry.

112
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
The log had been kept up through December
thirteenth.

113
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,375
Notes for the fourteenth and fifteenth, up
to nine in the morning, were written on a

114
00:05:31,375 --> 00:05:33,000
slate, waiting to be copied in.

115
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,875
Two sets of oilskins and sea boots were
missing, and Moore, who knew the routine,

116
00:05:39,875 --> 00:05:44,000
said the men wore those only when heading
down

117
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
to the landings.

118
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,250
The best conclusion was that two of the
keepers were swept from the west landing

119
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:53,000
while trying to secure equipment.

120
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
The register lists all three men as dead,
cause, probably drowning.

121
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Then the legend got to the file.

122
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,917
Years later, dramatic log entries entered
the story: a storm of terrible fury, the

123
00:06:03,917 --> 00:06:06,000
keepers praying, one of them weeping.

124
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Those entries are not part of the original
record.

125
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
They were added to the story long after
the men were gone.

126
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Three men, one island, and an ocean to
blame.

127
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
The next case had a beach.

128
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
December first, 1948. Somerton Beach,
south of Adelaide, Australia.

129
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
A well-dressed man is found slumped
against the seawall.

130
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,000
No wallet.

131
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,000
No identification.

132
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
The labels have been removed from his
clothing.

133
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:38,500
A suitcase linked to him turns up at the
railway station, and the labels are

134
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:41,000
missing from those clothes too.

135
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
It carries two printed words.

136
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Tamam Shud.

137
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Persian for ended, or finished.

138
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
The scrap had been torn from the last page
of a copy of the Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam.

139
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
A copy with that page missing had been
found in a car near the beach.

140
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Nearby, a string of letters that
investigators took for a code.

141
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
For more than seventy years, he had no
name.

142
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:07,958
Then, in July 2022, researchers at the
University of Adelaide, led by Professor

143
00:07:07,958 --> 00:07:13,000
Derek Abbott together with the genealogist
Colleen Fitzpatrick, announced a DNA

144
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:14,000
match.

145
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
Carl Charles Webb.

146
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Forty-three years old.

147
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
An electrical engineer from Melbourne.

148
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
The name solves one problem and creates
another.

149
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
A name does not explain the missing
labels.

150
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:30,542
It does not explain the torn page, or the
code, or why a man from Melbourne ended up

151
00:07:30,542 --> 00:07:32,000
on that particular beach.

152
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Getting the answer to who only made the
question of why louder.

153
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
At this point, a reasonable person might
say, so what.

154
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
Storms happen.

155
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:43,000
Panic happens.

156
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:44,000
A man can die on a beach.

157
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,000
True.

158
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,000
But the evidence does not stop there.

159
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
February 1959. The northern Ural
Mountains.

160
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:52,125
Nine experienced hikers, led by a
twenty-three-year-old named Igor Dyatlov,

161
00:07:52,125 --> 00:07:55,000
spend the night on a mountain slope.

162
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:01,000
On February twenty-sixth, searchers find
their tent, half buried in snow.

163
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
It has been cut open from the inside.

164
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Their supplies and warm clothing are still
in it.

165
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
Some of the hikers had fled into the snow
in their socks, or barefoot.

166
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,083
The search turns up the hikers over the
following weeks, at different distances

167
00:08:14,083 --> 00:08:15,000
from the camp.

168
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Some had injuries that investigators
struggled to explain.

169
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
For decades, the theories ran from the
military to the supernatural.

170
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Then, in January 2021, two Swiss
researchers, Alexander Puzrin and Johan

171
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Gaume, published a study in the journal
Communications Earth and Environment.

172
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Their model went like this.

173
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:40,000
The hikers had cut into the slope to pitch
their tent.

174
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Wind piled snow above them.

175
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:47,000
About nine hours later, a small slab
avalanche came down.

176
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,000
Not a huge one.

177
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,000
Just enough.

178
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
The tent was no longer safe, so they cut
their way out.

179
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,792
Russian authorities who reopened the case
also concluded an avalanche was the main

180
00:08:54,792 --> 00:08:55,000
cause.

181
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
It sounds like an ending.

182
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:02,500
Except the researchers themselves describe
what they offered as a plausible

183
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:05,000
explanation, not a closed case.

184
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Plausible.

185
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,000
That is a different word from solved.

186
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:13,000
And that is what brings the whole thing
into focus.

187
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:14,000
The earlier cases were never

188
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:15,000
isolated.

189
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:16,000
They were a pattern.

190
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
In every one, a perfectly ordinary
explanation exists.

191
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:21,000
A lifeboat.

192
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:22,000
A wave.

193
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,000
A fear.

194
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,000
A cut slope.

195
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:28,708
And in every one, the ordinary explanation
fits the facts, and the people are still

196
00:09:28,708 --> 00:09:29,000
gone.

197
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
One more.

198
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
And this one has a word carved into it.

199
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,375
In 1587, an artist named John White leads
a group of English settlers to Roanoke

200
00:09:37,375 --> 00:09:40,000
Island, off the coast of what is now North

201
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:41,000
Carolina.

202
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
About a hundred and fifteen men, women,
and children.

203
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Among them are his daughter, and his
newborn granddaughter, Virginia Dare.

204
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
The first English child born in the
Americas.

205
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,000
White sails back to England for supplies,
promising to return soon.

206
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Before he goes, he and the colonists agree
on a signal.

207
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:06,000
If they have to leave, they will carve
their destination.

208
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,000
If they leave in danger, they will carve a
small cross.

209
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Then England goes to war with Spain, and
every able ship is wanted for the fight.

210
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:18,000
White is stuck on the wrong side of the
ocean for three years.

211
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:21,000
He finally lands on Roanoke on August
eighteenth, 1590.

212
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
It is his granddaughter's third birthday.

213
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
The houses have been taken down.

214
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:27,000
The settlement is deserted.

215
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
On a post of the palisade, one word,
carved in capital letters.

216
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Croatoan.

217
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:38,000
The name of a nearby island, and of the
people who lived there.

218
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,000
And no cross.

219
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,000
No cross.

220
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Which means that whatever happened, it was
not carved in distress.

221
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
Which means White had every reason to
hope.

222
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Then the weather turns.

223
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
Rough seas.

224
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Lost anchors.

225
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
A fleet in no shape to search.

226
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:53,000
The ships turn back for England.

227
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:58,000
John White never sees his daughter or his
granddaughter again.

228
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Think about that.

229
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Not the mystery.

230
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:02,000
The man.

231
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,833
Three years of trying to get home, and a
birthday spent reading a single carved

232
00:11:04,833 --> 00:11:05,000
word.

233
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:10,000
One common reading is that the word was
simply a message.

234
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
Nothing cryptic about it.

235
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
It may be the most honest note in this
entire story.

236
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
It said exactly where they were going.

237
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
And it did not help anyone.

238
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
So what does all of this actually mean?

239
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
Look at what the legends did.

240
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:28,000
They gave the Mary Celeste a warm
breakfast.

241
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,000
They gave the lighthouse keepers a storm
and a prayer.

242
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
They gave Strasbourg a daily body count.

243
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Every time, someone looked at an empty
space and could not stand it.

244
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:42,000
The real record is thinner, and quieter.

245
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:45,000
And the gap is the point.

246
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,500
Most people never connect these dots
because each story seems to belong to a

247
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:49,000
different genre.

248
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:50,000
A ghost ship.

249
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,000
A medical oddity.

250
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
A lighthouse tale.

251
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:54,000
A cold case.

252
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:55,000
A hiking disaster.

253
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:56,000
A colonial footnote.

254
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Put them side by side and they are one
story about the same thing.

255
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
People can survive not knowing.

256
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
What they cannot survive is not having an
ending.

257
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,000
So the mind supplies one.

258
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Tea.

259
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Prayers.

260
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,000
A curse.

261
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:12,000
A monster.

262
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:13,000
The invention is not the creepy part.

263
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
The creepy part is how badly the invention
was needed.

264
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Six places.

265
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Six silences.

266
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:24,000
A ship, a street, a lighthouse, a beach, a
mountain, a post.

267
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
In each one, somebody was supposed to come
back.

268
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,000
In each one, somebody was supposed to
leave a note.

269
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
At the start, there was a simple idea.

270
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:35,000
Every culture agrees that somebody should
have left a note.

271
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,000
Now it looks like the note was never the
point.

272
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
The point is what happens when it goes
missing.

273
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,000
A tea is invented.

274
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
A storm is invented.

275
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:48,000
A ship's name is kept alive for a hundred
and fifty years.

276
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Maybe that is the real question underneath
all six.

277
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Why do we need someone to be coming back
for us?

278
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
And why, when nobody does, can we not stop
writing the note?

279
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Somewhere right now, someone is carving a
word into a post.

280
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,083
And hoping somebody comes back to read it.
